In both models, we denote the negative sample log-likelihood as $$R_n(\theta)=-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \log p_\theta(Y_i),$$ where $p_\theta(Y_i)$ is the Gaussian mixture density for $Y_i$, marginalizing over the unknown rotation $g_i \sim \Lambda$. This density is given in the projected setting by $$p_\theta(y)=\int_{\mathsf{G}} \frac{1}{(2\pi\sigma^2)^{\tilde{d}/2}}
\exp\left(-\frac{\|y-\Pi(g \cdot \theta)\|^2}{2\sigma^2}\right)\mathrm{d}
\Lambda(g),\tag{2.3}$$ and in the unprojected setting by the same expression with $\Pi=\mathop{\mathrm{Id}}$ and $\tilde{d}=d$.

We denote the negative *population* log-likelihood function by $$R(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[R_n(\theta)]=-\mathbb{E}[\log p_\theta(Y)],\tag{2.5}$$ where the expectation is taken under the true model $Y \sim p_{\theta_*}$. $R(\theta)$ depends implicitly on $\theta_*$, but we will omit this dependence in the notation.

We will restrict attention to projected models where $$\text{there are a finite number of orbits }
\mathcal{O}_{\theta} \text{ such that }
\Pi(\mathcal{O}_{\theta}) \equiv
\Pi(\mathcal{O}_{\theta_*}), \text{ for generic } \theta_* \in \mathbb{R}^d.\tag{2.4}$$

Turning to the forms of $s_k(\theta)$ that define the moment optimization (2.19), write $\theta_* \in \mathbb{R}^d$ for the true coefficients in the above real basis $\{h_{lsm}\}$. Let $$u^{(ls)}(\theta)=(u_m^{(ls)}(\theta):m=-l,\ldots,l) \in \mathbb{C}^{2l+1}\tag{4.8}$$ be the components of the *complex* coefficients $u=\hat{V}^*\theta$ for the frequency pair $(l,s)$, and define analogously to (4.2) $$B_{(l,s),(l',s'),(l'',s'')}(\theta)=
\mathop{\sum_{m=-l}^l \sum_{m'=-l'}^{l'} \sum_{m''=-l''}^{l''}}_{m''=m+m'}
\langle l,m;l',m'|l'',m'' \rangle
\overline{u_m^{(ls)}(\theta)u_{m'}^{(l's')}(\theta)}
u_{m''}^{(l''s'')}(\theta).\tag{4.9}$$

$$B_{l,l',l''}(\theta)=
\mathop{\sum_{m=-l}^l \sum_{m'=-l'}^{l'}
\sum_{m''=-l''}^{l''}}_{m''=m+m'}
\langle l,m;l',m'|l'',m'' \rangle
\overline{u_m^{(l)}(\theta)u_{m'}^{(l')}(\theta)} u_{m''}^{(l'')}(\theta)\tag{4.2}$$ where $\langle l,m;l',m'|l'',m'' \rangle \in \mathbb{R}$ is the Clebsch-Gordan coefficient.

We now extend the model of the preceding section to include the tomographic projection arising in cryo-EM. In this projected model, we observe samples $$(\Pi \cdot f_\mathfrak{g})(x)\mathrm{d}x+\sigma\,\mathrm{d}W(x)\tag{4.10}$$ on $\mathbb{R}^2$ where, for $x=(x_1,x_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2$, the tomographic projection $\Pi$ is defined by $$(\Pi \cdot f_\mathfrak{g})(x_1,x_2)=
\int_{-\infty}^\infty f_{\mathfrak{g}}(x_1,x_2,x_3)\mathrm{d}x_3,\tag{4.11}$$ and $\mathrm{d}W(x)$ in (4.10) is a standard Gaussian white noise process on the projected domain $\mathbb{R}^2$.

We again model the Fourier transform of $f$ in a basis $\{\hat{j}_{lsm}\}$ given by the product of complex spherical harmonics with radial functions. We restrict $f$ to a space of $(L,S_0,\ldots,S_L)$-bandlimited functions with representation $$f=\sum_{(l,s,m) \in \mathcal{I}} u_m^{(ls)} \cdot j_{lsm}
=\sum_{(l,s,m) \in \mathcal{I}} \theta_m^{(ls)} \cdot h_{lsm}\tag{4.12}$$ for the index set $\mathcal{I}$ defined in (4.5), where $\{j_{lsm}\}$ are the inverse Fourier transforms of $\{\hat{j}_{lsm}\}$, and the second equality describes a parametrization by an equivalent real orthonormal basis $\{h_{lsm}\}$ as before. In Appendix D.4, we apply the Fourier slice theorem to derive basis representations for the tomographic projection $\Pi \cdot f$. These take the forms $$\Pi \cdot f=\sum_{(s,m) \in \mathcal{\widetilde{I}}} \tilde{u}_m^{(s)} j_{sm}
=\sum_{(s,m) \in \mathcal{\widetilde{I}}} \tilde{\theta}_m^{(s)} h_{sm},\tag{4.13}$$ where $\{j_{sm}\}$ and $\{h_{sm}\}$ are (complex and real, resp.) basis functions over $\mathbb{R}^2$, and $\Pi \cdot f$ is bandlimited to an index set $$\mathcal{\widetilde{I}}=\Big\{(s,m):1 \leq s \leq S,\; -L \leq m \leq L\Big\},
\qquad \tilde{d}=|\mathcal{\widetilde{I}}|=S(2L+1)\tag{4.14}$$ for $S=\max(S_0,\ldots,S_L)$. This expresses $\Pi$ as a linear map from $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^d$ to $\tilde{\theta}\in \mathbb{R}^{\tilde{d}}$, and we give its explicit form in (D.57). We choose radial functions to ensure that the basis $\{h_{sm}\}$ is orthonormal in $L_2(\mathbb{R}^2)$, so that (4.10) is equivalent to observing the coefficients of $\Pi \cdot f$ in this basis with i.i.d. $\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2)$ noise. Further details of the setup are described in Appendix D.4.

